            <ArticleTitle>Circulating endothelial progenitor cells correlate with erectile function in patients with coronary heart disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2184-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIMS" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The aim of the study was to determine the influence of endothelial progenitor cells (EPC) on erectile dysfunction (ED). EPC play a major role in repair mechanisms of the endothelial monolayer, but the role of EPC in ED is unclear.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS AND RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Circulating levels of CD34(+)/KDR(+) and CD133(+) EPC were determined in 119 patients with known coronary artery disease. ED was evaluated with an ED-score generated from the KEED questionnaire. Prevalence of ED was 59.7%. In univariate analysis, age, hypertension, reduced left ventricular ejection fraction (LVEF), diabetes, and circulating levels of CD133(+) EPC, but not cardiovascular drug treatment were associated with ED. Body mass index (BMI) was positively (r = 0.319, P=0.003) and high-density lipoprotein was negatively (r=-0.246, P=0.034) correlated with ED. Adjustment for age, diabetes, hypertension, BMI, smoking, LVEF, use of statins and lower urinary tract symptoms, and prior coronary intervention revealed low levels of circulating immature CD133(+) EPC as independent risk factor for ED (95% CI -11.183 to -1.7371, P=0.008).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Reduced levels of circulating CD133(+) EPC are an independent risk factor for ED. Thus, EPC may be a link between cardiovascular risk factors, endothelial dysfunction, and ED.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
